    "selected_growth_rate": 3.0,
    "growth_rate_source": "judgment: revenue 10yr CAGR 1.8% and 5yr 5.9% (off the FY2020 COVID base) bracketed; ~3% = nominal GDP-like volume plus inflation-plus pricing",
    "growth_divergence_warning": "Equity CAGR (0.1% 5yr) diverges >30% from revenue (5.9%) and EPS (5.1%) CAGRs. Book value is not a growth measure here: CSX returns essentially all FCF via dividends and buybacks ($4.7B repurchased FY2022), so equity is flat by policy. EPS 5yr CAGR uses FY2020 EPS split-adjusted for the June-2021 3:1 split (3.61/3).",
    "notes": "Owner-FCF CAGRs (SBC deducted, no interest-income strip as net debt) track reported FCF closely because SBC is <0.4% of revenue. Both are strongly negative over 3 and 5 years because FY2022 FCF ($3.4B) was a peak (low capex, strong coal pricing) and FY2025 ($1.7B) a trough (hurricane/tunnel capex, weak pricing) -- neither end is a run-rate, which is why the forward build uses components rather than these CAGRs."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "CSX consolidates the 2026 operating recovery: the Blue Ridge (Helene) line and the Howard Street Tunnel double-stack clearance are finished, the post-2025 management change's cost programme holds EBITDA margin near 48%, and volumes grow with the eastern-US industrial economy plus modest intermodal share gains from trucks. Export coal drifts lower and is offset by merchandise pricing above rail inflation. The Union Pacific-Norfolk Southern combination (if approved by the STB) competes for some eastern single-line traffic but CSX keeps its franchise via interline partnerships (BNSF). Capex settles at ~15.5% of revenue.",
    "bull": "Precision-scheduled-railroading discipline returns CSX to peer-leading margins (EBITDA ~51%, operating ratio high-50s), Howard Street double-stack and I-95 corridor reshoring/nearshoring drive intermodal and merchandise growth of 4-5%, and capex intensity falls to ~14.5% once the catch-up projects roll off. Rail-merger consolidation (a BNSF or CPKC combination answering UP-NS) is optionality not modelled in the flows, but the growth path reflects the network gaining single-line reach through partnerships.",
    "bear": "An industrial/freight recession in 2027 flattens volumes, export and domestic coal (roughly an eighth of revenue) accelerates its decline, and a merged UP-NS transcontinental diverts eastern single-line intermodal and chemicals traffic. Service slips again (as in 2022 and 2025), cost savings leak back and EBITDA margin settles at ~45%; capex stays elevated at 16.5% for resilience (weather hardening, PTC/tech renewal). Terminal growth 2% and a 15x cap reflect a structurally slower franchise."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "A railroad is durable, hard-to-replicate infrastructure with pricing power, but it is also a heavy reinvester: capex has run 15-21% of revenue against D&A of ~11%, so owner FCF is materially below net income plus depreciation. The model values the unlevered owner FCF from a component build (EBITDA -> SBC -> D&A -> 24% cash tax on EBIT -> capex -> working capital) at an 8% USD WACC, deducts $17.5B net debt at the June 2026 balance sheet, and divides by a flat 1,859M diluted shares.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Capex intensity: each 1pp of revenue in perpetual capex is ~$150M/yr of FCF, roughly $1.4/share",
      "Margin durability of the 2026 cost programme after the 2025 CEO change",
      "UP-NS merger approval and its traffic diversion from eastern single-line competition",
      "Export coal price/volume (thermal and met) and industrial cycle",
      "Terminal value share is high for a low-growth, long-duration asset; the market price embeds a lower discount rate than 8%"
    ]
  },
